1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,740
On the evening of 22 October 1956, students in Budapest drew up a list of sixteen demands.

2
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:16,480
The first broke a taboo that even that year's boldest debates had kept: all Soviet troops out.

3
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:22,650
Three and a half years earlier, Hungary had been a land of internment camps, show trials and fear.

4
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:30,850
This is the road between, from Stalin's death in March 1953 to the eve of the revolution.

5
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:45,190
On 5 March 1953, Joseph Stalin died, and his heirs in Moscow wanted calm in the countries they controlled.

6
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:53,960
Hungary worried them. Since 1949, wage earners' real wages had fallen by a fifth, and the economy was near collapse.

7
00:00:54,040 --> 00:01:01,380
In June, they summoned Hungary's leaders to Moscow and condemned Mátyás Rákosi's policies to his face.

8
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:10,230
Moscow's choice for prime minister was Imre Nagy, the Communist who had carried out the land reform of 1945.

9
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:18,720
On 4 July 1953, Nagy announced a new course in Parliament. For once, the country heard it from the government.

10
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:25,390
The internment camps were closed, the deportations ended, and an amnesty set many prisoners free.

11
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:31,810
Money moved from heavy industry to food and housing, and peasants could leave the collective farms.

12
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:38,300
The party's own reports told of villages lighting bonfires and celebrating Nagy until dawn.

13
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:48,570
By 1954, people consumed a fifth more than in 1950, and the web of terror, as one historian wrote, began to come apart.

14
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:55,840
But Rákosi stayed First Secretary of the party, and the party still ran the press, the radio and the officials.

15
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:03,240
Hungary now had two centres of power, and from the first day, Rákosi worked to win his back.

16
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:13,550
Many victims of the show trials were freed, among them János Kádár, but László Rajk's case stayed taboo.

17
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:22,650
In Moscow in January 1955, Nagy said he could not work with Rákosi, and the Soviet leaders branded him anti-party.

18
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:29,870
In April, the party stripped him of his posts, and Rákosi's man, András Hegedüs, became prime minister.

19
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:36,940
Nagy refused to recant, and in December 1955 he was expelled from the party.

20
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:44,360
The old course returned: more for heavy industry, heavier deliveries, and new pressure to join the collective farms.

21
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:52,130
But the old fear did not. Prisoners kept coming home, and writers began to protest against the party's control.

22
00:02:52,210 --> 00:03:01,480
Next door, 1955 went the other way. On 14 May, Hungary signed the Warsaw Pact, an alliance under a Soviet commander.

23
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:08,220
The next day, the Austrian State Treaty ended the occupation of Austria, which soon declared itself neutral.

24
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:18,520
Since 1947, Soviet troops had stayed in Hungary to guard their lines to Austria. Many expected them to go, but they stayed.

25
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:31,030
On 25 February 1956, Nikita Khrushchev denounced Stalin's crimes in a secret speech to the Soviet party congress.

26
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:37,270
He spoke of the mass arrests and executions of party members, and of the cult of one leader.

27
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:45,260
For Rákosi, who had copied Stalin's cult in Hungary, it was a trap. He had to expose crimes he was responsible for.

28
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:53,860
In March, he had to admit that László Rajk, hanged in 1949, had been condemned on fabricated charges.

29
00:03:53,940 --> 00:04:01,210
Debate moved to the Petőfi Circle, a club of the party's youth league, named after the poet of 1848.

30
00:04:01,290 --> 00:04:08,330
On 18 June, Rajk's widow, Júlia, rose before the Circle and asked for her husband's name to be cleared.

31
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:16,250
On 27 June, a debate on the press filled the army officers' club, and loudspeakers carried it to the crowd outside.

32
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:25,320
About 6,000 people came, by the chairman's count. They stayed until half past one in the morning, and called for Nagy's return.

33
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:33,490
The Soviet ambassador, Yuri Andropov, reported that it had turned into a demonstration against the party leadership.

34
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:42,380
The next day, in Poznań in Poland, workers marched for better pay and conditions. Security forces fired, and dozens were killed.

35
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:50,210
On 30 June, Hungary's party leadership condemned the Petőfi Circle, and its debates stopped.

36
00:04:53,610 --> 00:05:01,070
Moscow feared trouble in Hungary too. In July, it sent one of its leaders, Anastas Mikoyan, to Budapest.

37
00:05:01,150 --> 00:05:08,760
On 18 July, Rákosi resigned, citing his health and his mistakes, and soon left for the Soviet Union.

38
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:15,780
His successor as First Secretary was Ernő Gerő, who had run the economy at Rákosi's side for years.

39
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:23,500
New men such as János Kádár joined the leadership, but the American Legation reported that the old gang was still there.

40
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:31,040
Gerő named the Petőfi Circle among the enemy's tools, yet his leadership no longer had the strength to crack down.

41
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:40,620
Life stayed hard. By a trade union survey, more than half of all families with three children lived below the subsistence minimum.

42
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:53,410
On 6 October 1956, the anniversary of the martyrs of Arad, four coffins stood in the Kerepesi cemetery in Budapest.

43
00:05:53,490 --> 00:06:01,630
László Rajk was reburied with three other victims of the same trials, Tibor Szőnyi, András Szalai and György Pálffy.

44
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:08,900
A huge crowd came. Estimates run from 100,000 to 350,000 people.

45
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:15,470
Béla Szász, once tried with Rajk, said they had come not only to honour the dead, but to bury an era.

46
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:23,370
Imre Nagy offered his sympathy to Júlia Rajk, and that afternoon, students marched through the city centre against Stalinism.

47
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:29,910
On 14 October, the party took Nagy back, but gave him no place in government.

48
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:39,530
On 16 October, students in Szeged left the party's youth league and founded their own union, MEFESZ, with leaders they chose themselves.

49
00:06:39,610 --> 00:06:46,750
On 19 October, Soviet leaders flew unannounced to Warsaw, where Poland's party was changing course.

50
00:06:46,830 --> 00:06:53,120
On 21 October, Władysław Gomułka was elected party leader there, and Moscow gave way.

51
00:06:53,200 --> 00:07:01,470
For Hungarians, the UN committee later wrote, this was the catalyst they had been half consciously waiting for.

52
00:07:04,870 --> 00:07:13,190
On Monday 22 October, more than 2,000 people filled the great hall of the Technical University in Budapest.

53
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:21,580
They voted to join MEFESZ, and through the evening their list of demands grew from ten points to fourteen, then sixteen.

54
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:27,480
Point one demanded the immediate withdrawal of all Soviet troops, under the peace treaty.

55
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:33,100
They wanted a new government under Imre Nagy, and free elections with several parties.

56
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:41,670
Rákosi was to be tried, the press and radio set free, the Stalin statue taken down, and the Kossuth coat of arms restored.

57
00:07:41,750 --> 00:07:49,790
The Petőfi Circle had never touched two taboos, the Soviet troops and the one-party state. The students broke both.

58
00:07:49,870 --> 00:07:56,610
That evening, the radio refused to broadcast the demands in full, and the students would not let them be cut.

59
00:07:56,690 --> 00:08:04,380
So the text was dictated into a microphone, written down in the hall, and copied by hand and typewriter across the city.

60
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:13,920
The Writers' Union meant to lay a wreath at the statue of General Bem, a Pole who fought for Hungary in 1848 and 1849.

61
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,590
The students called a silent march there for the next afternoon, in sympathy with Poland.

62
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:27,630
Three and a half years had led here: a new course and its fall, a secret speech, a funeral, and sixteen points.

63
00:08:27,710 --> 00:08:35,230
The country would not go on living the old way, a historian writes, and the regime no longer had the strength to defend it.

64
00:08:35,310 --> 00:08:42,410
The next afternoon, the students marched. What followed was a revolution and a freedom fight.
